"492",""
"493",""
"494",""
"495",""
"496",""
"497","On the authority of Abu al-Rudayn, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""No people gather together to study the Book of Allah, reciting it among themselves, except that they become guests of Allah, and the angels surround them until they rise or engage in talk other than it. And no one goes out seeking knowledge fearing that it may die out, or copies it fearing that it may be lost, except that he is like one who goes forth and returns in the path of Allah. And whoever is slowed down by his deeds, his lineage will not hasten him.""Narrated by al-Tabarani in al-Kabir, and in its chain is Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash, who is disputed regarding using him as evidence."
"498",""
"499",""
"500","On the authority of Sahl ibn Sa'd from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""Whoever enters this mosque of mine to learn good or teach it is like the mujahid in the cause of Allah, and whoever enters it for other than that, for the idle talk of people, is like the one who looks at what pleases him, yet it belongs to someone else."" Narrated by al-Tabarani in *al-Mu'jam al-Kabir*. In its chain is Ya'qub ibn Humayd ibn Kasib; al-Bukhari and Ibn Hibban authenticated him, while al-Nasa'i and others weakened him, basing his weakness only on the fact that he had received a legal punishment (hadd), though his transmission is sound."
"501",""
"502",""
"503","On the authority of Sakhbara, he said: ""Two men passed by the Messenger of Allah while he was sitting and engaged in remembrance, so he said: 'Sit down, for you two are upon goodness.' Then when the Messenger of Allah stood up and his companions dispersed from him, they stood up and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, you said to us: Sit down, for you two are upon goodness. Is this specifically for us or for people in general?' He said: 'There is no servant who seeks knowledge except that it becomes an expiation for what has preceded.'""I said: In al-Tirmidhi from it: ""Whoever seeks knowledge, it becomes an expiation for what has passed"" only.Al-Tabarani narrated it in al-Kabir, and in its chain is Abu Dawud al-A'ma, and he is a liar."
"504",""
"505","On the authority of Abu Hurayrah that he passed by the market of Medina and stopped by it. He said: ""O people of the market, how incapable you are!"" They said: ""And what is that, O Abu Hurayrah?"" He said: ""The inheritance of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) is being distributed and you are here. Will you not go and take your share of it?"" They said: ""And where is it?"" He said: ""In the mosque."" So they rushed out, and Abu Hurayrah waited for them until they returned. He said to them: ""What is the matter with you?"" They said: ""O Abu Hurayrah, we went to the mosque and entered it, but we did not see anything being distributed there."" Abu Hurayrah said to them: ""And did you not see anyone in the mosque?"" They said: ""Yes, we saw people praying, and people reciting the Qur'an, and people discussing the lawful and the unlawful."" Abu Hurayrah said to them: ""Woe to you! That is the inheritance of Muhammad (peace be upon him).""Narrated by al-Tabarani in al-Awsat, and its chain is good (hasan)."
"506",""
"507",""
"508",""
"509",""
"510",""
"511","On the authority of Ibn Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""The scholars of this nation are two men: a man whom Allah has given knowledge, so he conveys it to the people, not taking any worldly gain for it nor selling it for a price; for him, the whales of the sea, the beasts of the earth, and the birds in the sky seek forgiveness, and he will come to Allah as a noble master until he accompanies the messengers. And a man whom Allah has given knowledge, but he withholds it from the servants of Allah, takes worldly gain for it, and sells it for a price; that one will be bridled on the Day of Resurrection with a bridle of fire, and a caller will proclaim: 'This is the one whom Allah gave knowledge, but he withheld it from the servants of Allah, took worldly gain for it, and sold it for a price,' and so on until the reckoning is finished."" Al-Tabarani narrated it in Al-Awsat, and in its chain is Abdullah ibn Khirash; Al-Bukhari, Abu Zur'ah, Abu Hatim, and Ibn Adi weakened him, while Ibn Hibban deemed him trustworthy."
"512",""
"513",""
"514",""
"515",""
"516",""
"517",""
"518",""
"519",""
"520",""
"521",""
"522",""
"523",""
"524",""
"525",""
"526",""
"527",""
"528",""
"529","On the authority of Hizam ibn Hakim ibn Hizam, from his father, from the Prophet - may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him - who said: ""You have entered upon a time in which its jurists are many, its orators are few, its givers are many, its askers are few, and action in it is better than knowledge. And there will come a time in which its jurists are few, its orators are many, its askers are many, its givers are few, and knowledge in it is better than action.""Al-Tabarani narrated it in Al-Kabir, and in its chain is Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Tara'ifi, and he is trustworthy except that it was said about him: he narrates from weak narrators. And this is from his narration from Sadaqah ibn Khalid, and he is among the men of the Sahih."
"530",""
"531",""
"532",""
"533",""
"534","On the authority of Abu Malik al-Ash'ari, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) say: ""I do not fear for my Ummah except three things: that worldly provision becomes abundant for them, so they envy one another [and fight one another]; that the Book is laid open for a believer, and he seeks its interpretation, while none knows its interpretation except Allah, and those firmly grounded in knowledge say: 'We believe in it; all is from our Lord,' and none takes heed except those of understanding; and that they see their scholar, so they neglect him and do not care for him.""Al-Tabarani narrated it in Al-Mu'jam Al-Kabir, and its chain includes Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrating from his father, though he did not hear from his father."
"535",""
"536",""
"537",""
"538",""
"539",""
"540",""
"541",""
"542",""
"543","On the authority of Abu Umamah: A young man from Quraysh came to the Prophet (peace be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, give me permission to commit zina (fornication/adultery)."" The people turned to him and rebuked him, saying: ""Stop! Stop!"" The Prophet said: ""Bring him close."" So he came close to him. He said: ""Would you like it for your mother?"" He said: ""No, by Allah, may Allah make me a ransom for you."" He said: ""Nor do people like it for their mothers."" He said: ""Would you like it for your daughter?"" He said: ""No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, may Allah make me a ransom for you."" He said: ""Nor do people like it for their daughters."" He said: ""Would you like it for your sister?"" He said: ""No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, may Allah make me a ransom for you."" He said: ""Nor do people like it for their sisters."" He said: ""Would you like it for your paternal aunt?"" He said: ""No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, may Allah make me a ransom for you."" He said: ""Nor do people like it for their paternal aunts."" He said: ""Would you like it for your maternal aunt?"" He said: ""No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, may Allah make me a ransom for you."" He said: ""Nor do people like it for their maternal aunts."" Then he placed his hand on him and said: ""O Allah, forgive his sin, purify his heart, and guard his chastity."" After that, the young man never turned his attention to anything (of that nature).Narrated by Ahmad and al-Tabarani in al-Mu'jam al-Kabir, and its narrators are the narrators of the Sahih."
"544",""
"545",""
"546",""
"547","On the authority of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: “My brother Mūsā (peace be upon him) said: ‘O my Lord, show me the one whom You showed me on the ship.’ So Allah revealed to him: ‘O Mūsā, you will see him.’ He did not stay but a little while until al-Khiḍr came to him with a pleasant fragrance and wearing fine white garments. He said: ‘Peace be upon you, O Mūsā son of ʿImrān. Your Lord conveys His peace and the mercy of Allah to you.’ Mūsā said: ‘He is Peace, and from Him is Peace, and to Him is Peace. Praise be to Allah, Lord of the Worlds, whose blessings I cannot enumerate, nor can I give thanks for them except with His help.’ Then Mūsā said: ‘I want you to give me an admonition that Allah will benefit me with after you.’ Al-Khiḍr said: ‘O seeker of knowledge, the speaker is less weary than the listener, so do not bore"
"548",""
"549",""
"550","And from Safwan ibn Assal al-Muradi, who said: ""I came to the Prophet - may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him - while he was in the mosque, reclining on a red cloak of his. I said to him: O Messenger of Allah, I have come seeking knowledge. He said: 'Welcome to the seeker of knowledge! Indeed, the angels surround the seeker of knowledge with their wings, then they stack upon one another until they reach the lowest heaven, out of love for what he seeks.'""I said: He has another hadith with Abu Dawud and others, besides this one.Al-Tabarani narrated it in Al-Mu'jam Al-Kabir, and its narrators are the narrators of the Sahih."
"551",""
"552",""
"553",""
"554",""
"555","Qabisah ibn al-Mukhariq said: ""I came to the Prophet (peace be upon him) and he said to me: 'O Qabisah, what brought you here?' I said: 'I have grown old in age and my bones have become frail, so I came to you to teach me what Allah will benefit me with.' He said: 'O Qabisah, you have not passed by any stone, nor tree, nor clod of earth except that it sought forgiveness for you. O Qabisah, when you pray the dawn prayer and say three times: ""Glory be to Allah the Magnificent and with His praise,"" you will be protected from blindness, leprosy, and paralysis. O Qabisah, say: ""O Allah, I ask You from what is with You, and pour upon me from Your bounty, and spread upon me from Your mercy, and send down upon me from Your blessings.""'""Narrated by Ahmad, and in it is a man who was not named."
"556","On the authority of Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""There is no person who leaves his house except that two banners are at his door: a banner in the hand of an angel, and a banner in the hand of a devil. If he goes out for something that Allah loves, the angel follows him with his banner, and he remains under the banner of the angel until he returns to his house. And if he goes out for something that angers Allah, Mighty and Majestic is He, the devil follows him with his banner, and he remains under the banner of the devil until he returns to his house.""Narrated by Ahmad and al-Tabarani in *al-Awsat*. Its chain includes Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad; Malik deemed him trustworthy, while Ahmad and Yahya weakened him in one narration."
"557",""
"558",""
"559","On the authority of Ibn Abbas, he said: We were sitting with Abu Bakr al-Siddiq when a funeral procession passed by. He stood up, so we stood up. Then we prayed [the funeral prayer], and he removed his sandals. We said: ""O Caliph of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), you removed your sandals while people are wearing theirs?"" He said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) say: 'Whoever walks barefoot in obedience to Allah, Allah - Mighty and Majestic - will not ask him on the Day of Resurrection about what He made obligatory upon him.'""Al-Tabarani narrated it in Al-Awsat, and he said: It was narrated uniquely by Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Mu'awiyah al-Hadhdha'. I say: This Muhammad and his shaykh Abdullah ibn Ibrahim - I have not seen anyone who mentioned them both."
"560",""
"561","From Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: ""News reached me about a man who had heard a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him). So I bought a camel, then prepared my mount, and traveled to him for a month until I arrived in Sham. There was Abdullah ibn Unays. I said to the doorkeeper: 'Tell him Jabir is at the door.' He said: 'Ibn Abdullah?' I said: 'Yes.' So he came out dragging his garment, and he embraced me and I embraced him. I said: 'A hadith reached me from you that you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) regarding retribution, and I feared that you might die or I might die before I hear it.' He said: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) say: ""Allah will gather the people on the Day of Resurrection — or he said: the servants — naked, uncircumcised, buhman (destitute/with nothing)."" We said: ""And what is buhman?"" He said: ""They will have nothing with them. Then He will call them with a voice that those far away will hear just as those near will hear: 'I am the Judge, I am the Sovereign. It is not befitting for anyone of the people of Hell to enter Hell while he has a right due to him from anyone of the people of Paradise until I settle it for him from him. And it is not befitting for anyone of the people of Paradise to enter Paradise while anyone of the people of Hell has a right due to him from him until I settle it for him from him, even the slap.'"" We said: ""How is this when we only come naked, uncircumcised, buhman?"" He said: ""Good deeds and bad deeds.""'"" Narrated by Ahmad and al-Tabarani in al-Kabir, and Abdullah ibn Muhammad is weak."
"562","On the authority of Makhūl, that ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir came to Maslamah ibn Makhlad, and there had been some disagreement between him and the doorkeeper. He heard his voice and permitted him to enter. He said: ""I did not come to you as a visitor; I came to you for a need. Do you remember the day when the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'Whoever knows of a fault of his brother and conceals it, Allah will conceal his faults on the Day of Resurrection'?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""For this I came.""Al-Ṭabarānī narrated it in Al-Muʿjam al-Kabīr in this manner, and in Al-Muʿjam al-Awsaṭ on the authority of Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, who said: ""ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir went out..."" and he mentioned it in an abbreviated form. The narrators of Al-Kabīr are the narrators of the Ṣaḥīḥ."
"563","And from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr, from Munīb, from his uncle, he said: A man from the Companions of the Prophet — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him — [from a man from the Companions of the Prophet — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him —] was informed that he narrates from the Prophet — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him — that he said: ""Whoever covers (the faults of) his Muslim brother in this world, Allah will cover him on the Day of Resurrection."" And he traveled to him while he was in Egypt and asked him about the hadith. He said: Yes, I heard the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him — say: ""Whoever covers (the faults of) his Muslim brother in this world, Allah will cover him on the Day of Resurrection."" He said: So he said: And I also heard it from the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him.Narrated by Aḥmad. And this Munīb, if he is Ibn ʿAbdullāh, then Ibn Ḥibbān has authenticated him, and if he is someone else, then I have not seen anyone who mentioned him."
"564","Ibn Jurayj said: Abu Ayyub rode to 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir in Egypt. He said: ""I am going to ask you about a matter which none of those who witnessed it from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) remain except me and you. How did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) speak about covering (the faults of) a Muslim?"" He said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) say: 'Whoever covers a believer in this world regarding a fault, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will cover him on the Day of Resurrection.'"" So he returned to Medina and did not unpack his luggage until he narrated this hadith. Ahmad narrated it thus with a broken chain."
"565","On the authority of Raja' ibn Haywa, he said: I heard Maslama ibn Makhlad say: While I was in Egypt, the gatekeeper came to me and said: ""There is a Bedouin at the door on a camel seeking permission."" I said: ""Who are you?"" He said: ""Jabir ibn Abdullah al-Ansari."" He said: So I looked down at him and said: ""Shall I come down to you or will you come up?"" He said: ""Neither will you come down nor will I come up. A hadith has reached me that you narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) regarding covering a believer's fault, and I came to hear it."" I said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) say: ""Whoever covers a believer's fault, it is as if he has brought a buried-alive girl back to life."" So he struck his camel and returned.Al-Tabarani narrated it in Al-Awsat, and in its chain is Abu Sinan al-Qasmlani; Ibn Hibban and Ibn Khirash authenticated him in a narration, while Ahmad, Al-Bukhari, and Yahya ibn Ma'in weakened him."
"566",""
"567",""
"568",""
"569",""
"570",""
"571",""
"572",""
"573",""
"574",""
"575",""
"576","From ʿAṭāʾ, who said: I was with Ibn ʿAbbās when a man came to him and said: ""O Abū al-ʿAbbās, what do you say about me?"" He replied: ""And what could I possibly say about you?"" The man said: ""I am a scribe (ʿāmil bi-qalam)."" He said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'The owner of the pen will be brought on the Day of Resurrection in a coffin of fire, locked with locks of fire. [His pen will be examined as to in whose service he employed it.] If he employed it in obedience to Allah and His good pleasure, the coffin will be unlocked for him; but if he employed it in disobedience to Allah, he will be plunged in the coffin for seventy autumns until the pen nib and the inkwell straps rot away.'""Reported by al-Ṭabarānī in al-Awsaṭ and al-Kabīr. In its chain is Ayyūb al-Jīzī from Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAyyāsh. The apparent defect of this hadith is al-Jīzī, because al-Ṭabarānī said in al-Awsaṭ: ""Al-Jīzī narrated it uniquely."""
"577",""
"578",""
"579",""
"580",""
"581",""
"582","On the authority of Abū Saʿīd al-Khuḍrī, from the Prophet ﷺ, that he said during the Farewell Pilgrimage: ""May Allah brighten the face of a person who hears my statement, retains it, and conveys it; for perhaps a carrier of jurisprudence is not a jurist. Three things concerning which the heart of a believer does not harbor rancor: sincerity of action for Allah, sincere counsel for the leaders of the Muslims, and adherence to their main body (jamāʿah); for indeed their supplication encompasses from behind them.""Al-Bazzār narrated it, and its narrators are trustworthy, except that the shaykh of Sulaymān ibn Sayf might be Saʿīd ibn Bazyagh; for I have not seen anyone mention him. And if it is Saʿīd ibn al-Rabīʿ, then he is among the narrators of the Ṣaḥīḥ; for he (Sulaymān ibn Sayf) narrated from both of them. And Allah knows best."
"583",""
"584",""
"585","Mu'adh ibn Jabal said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""May Allah brighten a servant who hears my words, then does not add to them, for perhaps a carrier of understanding [conveys it] to one who comprehends it better. Three things the heart of a believer does not betray: sincerity of action for Allah, sincere advice to those in authority, and holding fast to the community of Muslims; for their supplication encompasses from behind them.""Recorded by al-Tabarani in al-Kabir and al-Awsat, except that in al-Awsat he said: ""Perhaps a carrier of a word"" instead of ""understanding."" It contains Amr ibn Waqid, who was accused of lying, and he is munkar al-hadith (rejected in narration)."
"586","On the authority of Al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, who said in a sermon: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) delivered a sermon to us in the Mosque of Al-Khaif and said: May Allah brighten the face of a servant who hears my words and conveys them; for perhaps a carrier of jurisprudence is not a jurist, and perhaps a carrier of jurisprudence conveys it to one who is more knowledgeable than him. Three things which the heart of a believer does not betray: sincerity of action for Allah, sincere advice to the rulers, and adherence to the community of the Muslims; for their supplication encompasses them from behind. Narrated by Al-Tabarani in Al-Kabir, and in its chain is Isa al-Khayyat, who is abandoned in hadith."
"587",""
"588","On the authority of Abu Qirṣāfa Jundara ibn Khaythama, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: “May Allah brighten a man who hears my words, comprehends them, and preserves them, for perhaps a carrier of knowledge conveys it to one more knowledgeable than him. Three things the heart does not betray: sincerity in action, sincere advice to the rulers, and adherence to the community.” He said: And it reached me that a son of Abu Qirṣāfa was captured by the Romans, so Abu Qirṣāfa would call out to him from the wall of Ascalon at the time of every prayer: “O so-and-so, the prayer!” And he would hear him and respond, while between them was the breadth of the sea. Reported by al-Ṭabarānī in al-Awsaṭ and al-Ṣaghīr, and its chain of narration — I have not seen anyone mention any of its narrators."
"589",""
"590",""
"591","On the authority of Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) delivered a sermon to us at the Mosque of al-Khaif in Mina, and he said: ""May Allah brighten the face of a person who hears my words, memorizes them, then conveys them to those who did not hear them. Perhaps a carrier of jurisprudence is not a jurist, and perhaps a carrier of jurisprudence conveys it to one who is more knowledgeable than him. Three things do not betray the heart of a believer: sincerity of action for Allah, sincere advice to those whom Allah has placed in authority over you, and adherence to the community of Muslims; for their supplication encompasses them from behind."" Narrated by al-Tabarani in al-Awsat, and in its chain is Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Aslam, and he is weak."
"592",""
"593","And on the authority of Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim, he said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) say at Al-Khayf — Khayf Minā: 'May Allah brighten a servant who hears my words, memorizes them, comprehends them, and conveys them to those who did not hear them. For perhaps a carrier of knowledge has no understanding himself, and perhaps a carrier of knowledge conveys it to one who has more understanding than him. Three things concerning which the heart of a believer does not harbor rancor: sincerity of action for Allah, sincere advice to the leaders of the Muslims, and adherence to their main body; for indeed their supplication encompasses from behind them.'""I say: Ibn Mājah narrated it in abbreviated form.Al-Ṭabarānī narrated it in Al-Muʿjam Al-Kabīr, and Aḥmad (narrated it). In its chain is Ibn Isḥāq from Al-Zuhrī, and he is a mudallis. And there is another route via Ṣāliḥ ibn Kaysān from Al-Zuhrī, and its narrators are trustworthy."
"594",""
"595","On the authority of Wabisah, that he used to stand before the people at al-Raqqah in the Great Mosque on the day of al-Fitr and the day of al-Nahr, and he said: ""I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) during the Farewell Pilgrimage while he was addressing the people, and he said: 'O people, which month is most sacred?' They said: 'This one.' He said: 'O people, which land is most sacred?' They said: 'This one.' He said: 'Then verily your blood, your wealth, and your honor are sacred to you like the sanctity of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours, until the day you meet your Lord. Have I conveyed (the message)?' The people said: 'Yes.' So he raised his hands (peace be upon him) to the sky and said: 'O Allah, bear witness.' Then he said: 'O people, let the witness among you convey to the absent.' So come near, let us convey to you as the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said to us."" Narrated by al-Bazzar, and its narrators are trustworthy."
"596",""
"597",""
"598",""
"599",""
"600",""
"601",""
"602","On the authority of Al-Muqanna', he said: ""I came to the Prophet (peace be upon him) with the zakat of our camels, so he ordered it to be collected. I said: 'There are two she-camels in it as a gift for you.' So he ordered the gift to be separated from the zakat. I stayed for some days, and people talked about the Messenger of Allah sending Khalid ibn al-Walid to the slaves of Egypt, or he said: Mudar — Abu Ghassan doubted — to collect zakat from them. I said: 'By Allah, we have no wealth, nor what is with our family, and he did not collect zakat from us here.' So I came to the Prophet (peace be upon him) while he was on a camel of his, with a black man whose head was level with the head of the Prophet (peace be upon him); I have not seen anyone among people taller than him. When I drew near, it was as if he leaned toward me, so the Prophet (peace be upon him) restrained him. I said: 'People have talked about such and such.' The Prophet (peace be upon him) raised his hands until I saw the whiteness of his armpits and said: 'O Allah, I do not permit them to lie against me.' Al-Muqanna' said: 'So I did not narrate from the Prophet (peace be upon him) except a hadith that was spoken by a Book or practiced by a Sunnah; they lie against him in his life, so how about after his death?'""Narrated by Al-Tabarani in Al-Kabir, and in it is Saif ibn Harun Al-Burjumi, and he is matruk (rejected)."
"603",""
"604",""
"605","On the authority of Ibn 'Abbas, that the Prophet - may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him - said: ""Advise one another sincerely in knowledge; for the betrayal of one of you in his knowledge is more severe than his betrayal in his wealth, and indeed Allah will question you on the Day of Resurrection.""Al-Tabarani narrated it in *Al-Mu'jam Al-Kabir*, and in its chain is Abu Sa'd Al-Baqqal. Abu Zur'ah said: ""He is weak in hadith, a mudallis (one who practices tadlis)."" It was said: ""Is he truthful?"" He said: ""Yes, he would not lie."" And Abu Hisham Al-Rifa'i said: ""Abu Usamah narrated to us, saying: Abu Sa'd Al-Baqqal narrated to us, and he was trustworthy."" Shu'bah, Al-Bukhari, and Yahya ibn Ma'in weakened him due to his tadlis, and the rest of the men in its chain are reliable (mawthaqun)."
"606",""
"607",""
"608","Muttarif said: ʿImrān ibn al-Ḥuṣayn said to me: ""O Muttarif, by Allah, I used to think that if I wished, I could narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) for two consecutive days without repeating a hadith. Then what increased my slowness in that and aversion to it was that men from the companions of Muhammad (peace be upon him) — or some of the companions of Muhammad (peace be upon him) — I witnessed as they witnessed, and heard as they heard, narrating hadiths that became ambiguous to them. So sometimes one would say: 'If I narrated to you that I heard the Prophet of Allah (peace be upon him) say such and such,' I saw that he had spoken truthfully. And sometimes he would swear an oath saying: 'I heard the Prophet of Allah (peace be upon him) say such and such.'""Narrated by Aḥmad, and in its chain is Abū Hārūn al-Ghanawī; I have not seen anyone who has given him a biographical notice.I say: And the hadith of ʿUmar comes in the chapter concerning whoever lied upon him (peace be upon him)."
"609",""
"610",""
"611",""
"612",""
"613",""
"614",""
"615",""
"616","On the authority of Dujayn Abu al-Ghusn, he said: I entered Medina and met Aslam, the freed slave of Umar ibn al-Khattab. I said: ""Narrate to me about Umar."" He said: ""I cannot, for I fear that I might add or omit."" We used to say to Umar: ""Narrate to us from the Messenger of Allah - peace be upon him."" He would say: ""I fear that I might add a letter or omit a letter. Indeed the Messenger of Allah - peace be upon him - said: 'Whoever lies about me, then he is in the Fire.'""Ahmad and Abu Ya'la narrated it, except that he (Abu Ya'la) said: ""Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire."" In it is Dujayn ibn Thabit Abu al-Ghusn, and he is weak, nothing (i.e., not reliable)."
"617","And from Uthman ibn Affan, that he used to say: ""Nothing prevents me from narrating from the Messenger of Allah - peace be upon him - except that I am not the most knowledgeable of his companions about him, but I bear witness that I heard him say: 'Whoever says about me what I did not say, let him take his seat in the Fire.'""And in a narration from Uthman ibn Affan, meaning he said: The Messenger of Allah - peace be upon him - said: ""Whoever says a lie about me, let him take a house in the Fire.""Both were narrated by Ahmad, Abu Ya'la, and al-Bazzar.And in al-Bazzar's narration: The Messenger of Allah - peace be upon him - said: ""Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire.""And similarly Abu Ya'la, and it is a hadith whose narrators are the narrators of the Sahih, and the first chain contains Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad, and he is weak, but he has been deemed trustworthy."
"618",""
"619",""
"620",""
"621",""
"622",""
"623",""
"624",""
"625","On the authority of Yahya ibn Maymun al-Hadrami, that Abu Musa al-Ghafiqi heard Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani narrating hadiths on the pulpit from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him). Abu Musa said: ""Your companion here is either a preserver (of hadith) or doomed. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) last enjoined upon us, saying: 'Adhere to the Book of Allah, and you will return to a people who love to narrate hadith about me. Whoever says about me what I did not say, let him take his seat in the Fire. And whoever preserves something, let him narrate it.'""Narrated by Ahmad, al-Bazzar, and al-Tabarani in al-Kabir, and its narrators are trustworthy."
"626","On the authority of Hisham ibn Abi Ruqayyah, who said: I heard Maslamah ibn Makhlad while he was standing on the pulpit addressing the people, saying: ""O people, do you not have in striped cloth and linen what suffices you instead of silk? And here is a man among you who informs you about the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him). Stand up, O 'Uqbah."" So 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir stood up and said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) say: 'Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire.' And I bear witness that I heard him say: 'Whoever wears silk in this world will be deprived of wearing it in the Hereafter.'"" Narrated by Ahmad, al-Tabarani in al-Kabir, and Abu Ya'la, and their narrators are trustworthy."
"627","On the authority of Abu Hayyan al-Taymi, from his uncle, who said: I set out, along with Husayn ibn Sabrah and Umar ibn Muslim, to Zayd ibn Arqam. When we sat with him, Husayn said to him: ""You have indeed encountered much good, O Zayd."" Yazid ibn Hayyan said: Zayd narrated to us in that gathering of his, saying: ""Abdullah ibn Ziyad sent for me, so I came to him. He said: 'What are these hadiths that you narrate and transmit from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) that we do not find in the Book of Allah? You narrate that he has a Pool in Paradise.' He said: 'The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) narrated it to us and promised it to us.' He said: 'You have lied, but you are an old man who has become senile.' He said: 'My two ears heard it, and my heart retained it from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), saying: ""Whoever lies upon me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire,"" and I have not lied upon the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him).' Narrated by Ahmad, al-Tabarani in al-Kabir, and al-Bazzar, and its narrators are the narrators of the Sahih."
"628",""
"629",""
"630",""
"631",""
"632",""
"633","Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah said: I went out with my father to a brother-in-law of ours from those who accepted Islam from the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ. I heard him saying: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: 'Relieve us with it, O Bilal' — meaning the prayer."" He said: I said: ""Did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?"" He became angry, and turned to narrate to them that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent a man to a tribe from the Arab tribes. When he came to them, he said to them: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded me to judge among your women as I wished."" They said: ""We hear and obey the command of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" And they sent a man to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who said: ""So-and-so came to us and said: The Prophet ﷺ commanded me to judge among your women. If this was by your command, then we hear and obey; and if it was otherwise, we wanted to inform you."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ became angry and sent a man from the Ansar, and said: ""Go to so-and-so and kill him, and burn him with fire."" He reached him and found he had died and been buried. He ordered him to be exhumed, then burned him with fire. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever lies against me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire."" He said: ""Do you see me lying against the Messenger of Allah ﷺ after this?"""
"634","On the authority of Abdullah ibn Amr: ""A man wore a suit resembling the suit of the Prophet — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him — then he came to a household in Medina and said: 'The Prophet — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him — commanded me that whichever household I wished, I could spy on.' They said: 'Our experience with the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him — is that he does not command indecencies.' He said: So they prepared a house for him and sent a messenger to the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him — and informed him. He said to Abu Bakr and Umar: 'Go to him; if you find him alive, kill him, then burn him with fire. And if you find him [dead], then you have been spared him, and I do not see you except that you have been spared him, so burn him.' They came to him and found he had gone out at night to urinate when a viper snake bit him and he died. So they burned him with fire. Then they returned to the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him — and informed him of the news. The Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him — said: 'Whoever lies against me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire.'""Tabarani narrated it in Al-Awsat, and in it is Ata ibn al-Sa'ib who became confused (in his later narrations). And Bukhari and Tirmidhi extracted from it: ""Whoever lies against me"" the hadith."
"635",""
"636",""
"637",""
"638",""
"639",""
"640",""
"641",""
