"652",""
"653","On the authority of Abu Umamah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat between the two eyes of Hell."" That was difficult for his companions, so they said: ""O Messenger of Allah, we narrate the hadith and we add and subtract."" He said: ""I do not mean you; rather I mean the one who lies about me deliberately, seeking to disgrace Islam."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, you said 'between the two eyes of Hell,' and does Hell have two eyes?"" He said: ""Yes, have you not heard Allah the Exalted say: 'When it sees them from a far place'? So does it see them except with two eyes?"" Al-Tabarani narrated it in Al-Mu'jam Al-Kabir, and in its chain is Al-Ahwas ibn Hakim. Al-Nasa'i and others weakened him, while Al-'Ijli and Yahya ibn Sa'id Al-Qattan authenticated him in a narration. And Muhammad ibn Al-Fadl ibn 'Atiyyah narrated from Al-Ahwas, and he is weak."
"654",""
"655",""
"656",""
"657",""
"658","On the authority of Abu Khaldah, he said: I heard Maimun al-Kurdi while he was with Malik ibn Dinar, so Malik ibn Dinar said to him: ""Why does the shaykh not narrate from his father, for your father reached (the time of) the Prophet - peace be upon him - and heard from him?"" He said: ""My father used not to narrate to us from the Prophet - peace be upon him - for fear that he might add or omit (something). And he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah - peace be upon him - say: 'Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire.'"" Narrated by al-Tabarani in al-Awsat, and its chain is good (hasan), Allah willing."
"659",""
"660",""
"661",""
"662",""
"663",""
"664",""
"665",""
"666","On the authority of Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb sent for Ibn Masʿūd, Abū Masʿūd al-Anṣārī, and Abū al-Dardāʾ, and said: ""What is this ḥadīth that you narrate abundantly from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?"" So he detained them in Medina until he was martyred.Al-Ṭabarānī narrated it in *al-Awsaṭ*. I say: This is a *munqaṭiʿ* (disconnected) report. Ibrāhīm was born in the year 20 AH, and he only experienced three years of ʿUmar's life. Ibn Masʿūd was in Kūfah. This is not authentic from ʿUmar.I say: The chapter on verification (*al-tathabbut*) regarding some ḥadīth will follow."
"667",""
"668",""
"669","Mubarak ibn Fadalah narrated: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim or Ibrahim ibn Isma'il stood up before al-Hasan and said: ""O Abu Sa'id, we hear from you hadiths that you narrate about the Messenger of Allah - peace be upon him - so provide us with their chains of transmission."" He said: ""Ask about whatever you wish."" He said: ""The hadith of the Prophet - peace be upon him - regarding the establishment of the Hour."" He said: ""Anas ibn Malik narrated to me from the Prophet - peace be upon him - and Jabir ibn Abdullah narrated to me from the Prophet - peace be upon him - and Abdullah ibn Qudamah narrated to me - and he was a man of truth - from al-Aswad ibn Sari' from the Prophet - peace be upon him."" They said: So they stood up and said: ""We almost overpowered this shaykh.""Al-Bazzar narrated it thus, and in its chain is Mubarak ibn Fadalah, and he is trustworthy but a mudallis."
"670","On the authority of Ibn Abbas and Ibn Umar, they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) came out to us with his head bandaged, then he ascended the pulpit and said: ""What are these books that reach me that you are writing? Books alongside the Book of Allah? Soon Allah will become angry for His Book, so it will be taken away at night; not leaving in a page nor in a heart of it a single letter except He will take it away."" Then some of those present in the gathering said: ""How about the believers, men and women, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Whoever Allah intends good for, He will keep in his heart: There is no god but Allah.""Narrated by Al-Tabarani in Al-Awsat, and in it is Isa ibn Maymun Al-Wasiti, and he is matruk (abandoned/weak), and Hammad ibn Salamah has authenticated him."
"671",""
"672","On the authority of Abu Sa'id — meaning al-Khudri — he said: ""We were sitting writing down what we heard from the Prophet — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him — so he came out to us and said: 'What is this you are writing?' We said: What we hear from you. He said: 'A book alongside the Book of Allah? Purify the Book of Allah and make it exclusive.' He said: So we gathered what we had written in one place, then we burned it with fire. We said: O Messenger of Allah, shall we narrate from you? He said: 'Yes, narrate from me and there is no harm, and whoever lies against me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire.' He said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, shall we narrate from the Children of Israel? He said: 'Yes, narrate from the Children of Israel and there is no harm, for you will not narrate anything from them except that there was something more wondrous than it among them.'""I say: It has a hadith in the Sahih with a different context.Ahmad narrated it, and in its chain is Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Aslam, and he is weak, and the rest of its narrators are narrators of the Sahih."
"673",""
"674","On the authority of Abu Burdah ibn Abi Musa, he said: I wrote a document from my father, so he said: ""Were it not that it contains the Book of Allah, I would have burned it."" Then he called for a vessel or a wide bowl, washed it (the writing), then said: ""Preserve from me what you heard from me, and do not write from me; for I did not write any book from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him). You almost destroyed your father.""Al-Tabarani narrated it in Al-Kabir and Al-Bazzar similarly, except that Al-Bazzar said: ""Preserve as we preserved from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)."" And its narrators are the narrators of the Sahih."
"675",""
"676","On the authority of Abu Hurairah, he said: ""No one was more knowledgeable of the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) than me, except for Abdullah ibn Amr; for he used to write it with his hand and preserve it in his heart, while I used to preserve it in my heart but not write it with my hand. And he sought permission from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) to write from him, so he permitted him.""Ahmad narrated it. And in the Sahih collections there is part of it with a different wording, excluding his seeking permission for writing and other matters. And it is from the narration of Ibn Ishaq from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, and Ibn Ishaq is a mudallis (one who practices tadlis), and Amr has some criticism regarding him."
"677",""
"678","On the authority of Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: ""There were some people with the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) from his companions, and I was with them, and I was the youngest of the group. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire.' When the people left, I said: 'How do you narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) when you have heard what he said, yet you are engrossed in narrating hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)?' They laughed and said: 'O son of our brother, indeed everything we heard from him is recorded with us in a book.'""Narrated by al-Tabarani in al-Kabir, and in its chain is Ishaq ibn Yahya ibn Talha, and he is abandoned in hadith."
"679",""
"680",""
"681",""
"682",""
"683",""
"684","Zayd ibn Thabit said: ""I used to write down the revelation for the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him). When revelation descended upon him, he would be seized by severe trembling, and he would sweat profusely like pearls. Then it would pass from him, and I would enter upon him with a shoulder bone piece or a fragment, and I would write while he dictated to me. I would not finish until my leg would nearly break from the weight of the Quran, until I would say: 'I will never walk on my leg again.' Then when I finished, he would say: 'Recite,' and I would recite it to him. If there was any omission, he would correct it, then I would go out with it to the people.""Al-Tabarani narrated it in Al-Awsat, and its narrators are trustworthy, except that it contains: ""I found in my maternal uncle's book."" So it is a wijada (transmission via written document)."
"685",""
"686","On the authority of Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr: ""The Prophet (peace be upon him) employed Abdullah ibn al-Arqam as a scribe, so he would write to kings. His trustworthiness with him reached such a degree that when he would write to some kings, he would write, then order that it be sealed, then seal it, not reading it due to his trustworthiness with him. He also employed Zayd ibn Thabit as a scribe, so he would write the revelation and write to kings as well. So when Abdullah ibn al-Arqam and Zayd ibn Thabit were absent and he needed to write to some commanders of armies and kings, and write a letter for a person to execute, he would order whoever was present to write. And Umar ibn al-Khattab, Uthman ibn Affan, Ali ibn Abi Talib, Zayd ibn Thabit, al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, Mu'awiyah ibn Abi Sufyan, Khalid ibn Sa'id ibn al-'As, and others among the Arabs who have been named wrote for him."" Al-Tabarani narrated it in Al-Mu'jam Al-Kabir, and in its chain is Salamah ibn al-Fadl al-Abrash. Al-Bukhari, Ibn al-Madini, and Abu Zur'ah weakened him, while Ibn Ma'in and Abu Hatim authenticated him."
"687",""
"688",""
"689",""
"690",""
"691",""
"692",""
"693",""
"694",""
"695",""
"696",""
"697",""
"698","On the authority of Abu Hazim, from Sahl, that he was in a gathering of his people while he was narrating to them about the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), and some of them were turning to each other conversing. So he became angry and then said: ""Look at them; I am narrating to them about the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) concerning what my eyes have seen and my ears have heard, yet some of them are turning to each other. By Allah, I will surely depart from among you and never return to you ever."" I said to him: ""Where are you going?"" He said: ""I am going to strive in the path of Allah."" I said: ""You have no capacity for jihad; you cannot hold onto a horse, nor can you strike with a sword, nor can you thrust with a spear."" He said: ""O Abu Hazim, I will go and be in the ranks, so that a stray arrow or a stone may come to me, and Allah may grant me martyrdom."" [He said: So he went, by my life, and he did not return except wounded.] Narrated by al-Tabarani in al-Kabir, and in its chain is Abd al-Hamid ibn Sulaiman, and he is weak."
"699","On the authority of Khalid ibn al-Walid, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""O Khalid, announce to the people: The prayer is congregational; none enters Paradise except a Muslim soul."" Then he went out and prayed at midday, then he stood among the people and said: ""I do not make lawful the wealth of the covenanted people without its due right. Perhaps a man among you says while reclining on his couch: 'What we found in the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, of lawful we made lawful, and what we found of unlawful we made unlawful.' Behold, I forbid you the wealth of the covenanted people without its due right.""Narrated by al-Tabarani in al-Kabir, and Abu Dawud narrated a portion of it, and in its chain is Baqiyyah, and he is weak."
"700",""
"701",""
"702",""
"703","On the authority of Al-Miqdād—meaning Ibn Al-Aswad—he said: I said to the Prophet ﷺ: ""I heard something from you about which I had doubts."" He said: ""If any of you doubts a matter, let him ask me about it."" I said: ""Your statement concerning your wives: 'I do not hope for them after me the ṣiddīqīn.'"" He said: ""And who do you consider the ṣiddīqīn?"" We said: ""Our children who die young?"" He said: ""No, the ṣiddīqīn are the charitable ones""—three times.Al-Ṭabarānī narrated it in Al-Kabīr, and its narrators are all trustworthy, except for Qurayba; Al-Dhahabī said: Her nephew Mūsā ibn Yaʿqūb Al-Zamʿī narrated exclusively from her.I say: Other ḥadīths on this meaning will appear in the chapter on asking about fiqh."
"704","On the authority of Abu al-Darda', Abu Umama, Wathila ibn al-Asqa', and Anas ibn Malik, they said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) came out to us one day while we were disputing about something concerning the religion. He became intensely angry, the like of which he had never become angry before. Then he rebuked us, saying: 'Gently, O community of Muhammad! Those before you were destroyed only by this. Abandon disputation, for its good is little. Abandon disputation, for the believer does not dispute. Abandon disputation, for the disputant's loss is complete. Abandon disputation, for it is sin enough that you remain a disputant. Abandon disputation, for the disputant — I will not intercede for him on the Day of Resurrection. Abandon disputation, for I am a guarantor of three houses in Paradise: in its outskirts, its middle, and its highest — for whoever abandons disputation while being truthful. Abandon disputation, for the first thing my Lord forbade me after the worship of idols was disputation [and drinking wine. Abandon disputation, for Satan has despaired of being worshipped, but he is pleased with you by incitement, and that is disputation. Abandon disputation], for the Children of Israel split into seventy-one sects, and the Christians into seventy-two sects, all of them in error except the great majority.' They said: 'O Messenger of Allah, what is the great majority?' He said: 'Whoever is upon what I and my companions are upon, whoever does not dispute in the religion of Allah and does not declare any of the people of monotheism an unbeliever because of a sin — he will be forgiven.' Then he said: 'Islam began as something strange and will return as something strange.' They said: 'O Messenger of Allah, who are the strangers?' He said: 'Those who rectify themselves when people become corrupt, and do not dispute in the religion of Allah, and do not declare any of the people of monotheism an unbeliever because of a sin.'""Narrated by al-Tabarani in al-Mu'jam al-Kabir, and in it is Kathir ibn Marwan, who is very weak."
"705",""
"706",""
"707",""
"708",""
"709",""
"710",""
"711",""
