"736",""
"737",""
"738","On the authority of Ibn 'Umar, who said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab said to 'Ali ibn Abi Talib: ""O Abu al-Hasan, sometimes you were present and we were absent, and sometimes we were present and you were absent. Three things I ask you about: do you have knowledge of any of them?"" 'Ali said: ""And what are they?"" He said: ""A man loves another man but has not seen any good from him, and a man hates another man but has not seen any evil from him?"" He said: ""Yes; the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'Verily, souls are recruited troops; they meet and sniff each other out; whatever recognizes each other harmonizes, and whatever denies each other diverges.'"" He said: ""One.""And he said: ""A man narrates a hadith, then forgets it, then remembers it?"" 'Ali said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) say: 'There is no heart but that it has a cloud like the cloud of the moon; while the moon shines, a cloud covers it and it darkens; when the cloud clears from it, it shines again. And while a man narrates a hadith, a cloud covers him and he forgets; when the cloud clears from him, he remembers.'"" 'Umar said: ""Two.""He said: ""And a man sees a dream, some of which come true and some of which lie?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) say: 'No servant, male or female, sleeps a heavy sleep except that his soul is ascended to the Throne. The one that does not wake up except at the Throne—that is the dream that comes true. And the one that wakes up before the Throne—that is the dream that lies.'"" So 'Umar said: ""Three things I was seeking; praise be to Allah who granted me them before death.""Al-Tabarani narrated it in Al-Awsat, and in it is Azhar ibn 'Abdullah. Al-'Uqayli said: ""His hadith is not preserved from Ibn 'Ajlan."" And this hadith is known from the hadith of Isra'il from Abu Ishaq, from Al-Harith, from 'Ali as mawquf (stopping at the Companion), and the rest of its narrators are trustworthy."
"739",""
"740","On the authority of Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, who said: I entered upon the Prophet (peace be upon him) and he said: ""O Ibn Mas'ud, what is the firmest handhold of faith?"" I said: ""Allah and His Messenger know best."" He said: ""The firmest handhold of Islam is: allegiance for the sake of Allah, love for the sake of Allah, and hatred for the sake of Allah."" Then he said: ""O Ibn Mas'ud."" I said: ""At your service, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""Do you know which people are best?"" I said: ""Allah and His Messenger know best."" He said: ""Indeed, the best of people are the best of them in deeds when they have understanding of their religion."" Then he said: ""O Ibn Mas'ud."" I said: ""At your service, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""Do you know which people are most knowledgeable?"" I said: ""Allah and His Messenger know best."" He said: ""Indeed, the most knowledgeable of people is the one most discerning of the truth when people differ, even if he is deficient in his deeds, and even if he crawls on his buttocks crawling. And those before you split into seventy-two sects; three were saved and the rest perished: a group that angered the kings, so they fought them over their religion and the religion of Jesus son of Mary (peace be upon him), so they seized them, killed them, and sawed them with saws; and a group that had no power to confront the kings, nor to remain among them calling them to the religion of Allah and the religion of Jesus, so they roamed the lands and became monks, and they are those about whom Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: {And monasticism they invented - We did not prescribe it for them except seeking Allah's pleasure} [Al-Hadid: 27] the verse."" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""So whoever believes in me, follows me, and believes me has truly observed it with its due observance, and whoever does not follow me, those are the perished ones."" Narrated by Al-Tabarani in Al-Awsat and Al-Saghir, and in it is Aqil ibn Al-Ja'd; Al-Bukhari said: Munkar (rejected) hadith."
"741",""
"742",""
"743",""
"744",""
"745",""
"746",""
"747",""
"748","On the authority of Alqamah ibn Sa'd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) delivered a sermon one day, praised certain groups of Muslims, then said: ""What is the matter with people who do not teach their neighbors, nor instruct them, nor admonish them, nor command them, nor forbid them? And what is the matter with people who do not learn from their neighbors, nor gain understanding, nor take heed? By Allah, a people shall teach their neighbors, instruct them, admonish them, command them, and forbid them; and a people shall learn from their neighbors, gain understanding, and become discerning, or I shall hasten punishment upon them."" Then he descended. Some people said: ""Who do you think he meant by these?"" He said: ""The Ash'aris. They are a people of understanding, and they have rough neighbors from the people of the waters and the Bedouins."" This reached the Ash'aris, so they came to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, you mentioned a people with good, and you mentioned us with evil, so what is our case?"" He said: ""A people shall teach their neighbors, instruct them, make them discerning, command them, and forbid them; and a people shall learn from their neighbors, become discerning, and gain understanding, or I shall hasten punishment upon them in this world."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, do we make others discerning?"" He repeated his statement to them, and they repeated their statement: ""Do we make others discerning?"" He said that also. They said: ""Give us a year's respite."" So he gave them a year's respite to instruct them, teach them, and make them discerning. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) recited this verse: {Those who disbelieved among the Children of Israel were cursed by the tongue of David...} [Al-Ma'idah: 78] the verse.Al-Tabarani narrated it in Al-Kabir, and in it is Bukayr ibn Ma'ruf. Al-Bukhari said: ""Discard him."" Ahmad authenticated him in one narration and weakened him in another. Ibn Adi said: ""I hope there is no harm in him."""
"749",""
"750",""
"751",""
"752",""
"753",""
"754","On the authority of Umm Sulaym, she said: ""I was staying with Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet (peace be upon him). Umm Sulaym said: 'O Messenger of Allah, what do you think? If a woman sees in a dream that her husband has had intercourse with her, must she perform ritual bathing (ghusl)?' Umm Salamah said: 'May your hands be rubbed with dust, O Umm Sulaym! You have shamed the women before the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him).' Umm Sulaym said: 'Indeed, Allah is not shy of the truth. It is better for us to ask the Prophet (peace be upon him) about what is unclear to us than to remain in ignorance about it.' The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'May your hands be rubbed with dust, O Umm Sulaym! Upon her is ritual bathing if she finds the fluid (water).' Umm Salamah said: 'O Messenger of Allah, does a woman have such fluid?' The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'Then how does her child resemble her? They (women) are the counterparts of men.'""Narrated by Ahmad, and it is in the Sahih in abbreviated form. In Ahmad's chain of narration, there is a break between Umm Sulaym and Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah. Further hadiths on this topic will follow in the chapters on purification and wet dreams, Allah willing."
"755","Ibn 'Umar said: ""I lived for a portion of time, and one of us would be granted faith before the Qur'an. A surah would be revealed to Muhammad (peace be upon him), so he would learn its lawful and unlawful matters, and what he should pause at from it, just as you learn the Qur'an. Then I have seen men to whom one of them is granted the Qur'an before faith, so he recites from the opening of the Book to its conclusion, not knowing what is its command nor its prohibition, nor what he should pause at from it, and he scatters it like the scattering of dates.""Narrated by al-Tabarani in al-Awsat, and its narrators are the narrators of the Sahih."
"756",""
"757",""
"758",""
"759",""
"760",""
"761",""
"762",""
"763",""
"764",""
"765",""
"766",""
"767",""
"768",""
"769",""
"770","On the authority of Hudhayfah, he said: ""A man asked (for charity) during the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), so the people withheld (from giving). Then a man gave him (something), so the people gave (as well). The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'Whoever initiates a good practice that is followed, he will have its reward and the reward of those who follow it, without that diminishing anything from their rewards. And whoever initiates an evil practice that is followed, he will bear its sin and the sin of those who follow it, without that diminishing anything from their sins.'""Narrated by Ahmad, al-Bazzar, and al-Tabarani in al-Awsat. Its narrators are the narrators of the Sahih, except for Abu Ubaydah ibn Hudhayfah, and Ibn Hibban has declared him trustworthy."
"771","On the authority of Abu Juhayfa, he said: A delegation of Abd al-Qays came to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) wearing striped cloaks, and the signs of hardship were upon them. He was displeased by what he saw of their condition, so he entered his house then came out, and he commanded charity and urged it. Then he said: ""Let a man give charity from a sa' of his wheat, and let him give charity from a sa' of his dates."" He said: A man came with a bundle and placed it, then people followed one after another until a collection of clothes and food gathered. He said: The face of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) lit up until it was as if it were a piece of gold. Then he said: ""Whoever establishes a good practice that is acted upon after him, he will have its reward and the reward of those who act upon it, without that diminishing anything from their rewards. And whoever establishes a bad practice that is acted upon after him, he will bear its sin and the sin of those who act upon it, without that diminishing anything from their sins.""I said: At Ibn Majah there is a part of it.Al-Tabarani narrated it in Al-Awsat, and in its chain is Ghassan ibn al-Rabi'. Ibn Hibban authenticated him, and Al-Daraqutni and others weakened him."
"772",""
"773",""
"774",""
"775","On the authority of Bishr ibn ʿUbayd Allāh — and he was an elderly shaykh — he said: We were with Ṭāwūs at the Maqām when he said, “What is this?” It was said, “A group of people whom Ibn Hishām seized for some cause and put iron collars on them.” So I heard Ṭāwūs narrating from Ibn ʿAbbās that the Messenger of Allāh (peace be upon him) said: “Whoever introduces an innovation in this Ummah will not die until that same innovation afflicts him.” Bishr ibn ʿUbayd Allāh said: I myself saw Ibn Hishām when he was dismissed, and the officials of al-Walīd ibn ʿAbd al-Malik came and put an iron collar on him.Al-Ṭabarānī narrated it in al-Awsaṭ, and in its chain is Bishr ibn ʿUbayd Allāh; Ibn Ḥibbān said: “Munkar al-ḥadīth.”"
"776",""
"777",""
"778",""
"779",""
"780","On the authority of Jubayr ibn Mut'im, he said: ""We were with the Prophet - may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him - at Al-Juhfah, when he said: 'Do you not bear witness that there is no deity but Allah alone, with no partner, and that I am the Messenger of Allah, and that the Quran has come from Allah?' We said: 'Yes.' He said: 'Then rejoice; for the Quran has one end in Allah's Hand and the other end in your hands, so hold fast to it, for you will never perish nor go astray after it ever.'""Narrated by Al-Bazzar and Al-Tabarani in Al-Kabir and Al-Saghir, and in its chain is Abu Ubaydah Al-Zuraqi, and he is abandoned in hadith."
"781",""
"782","On the authority of Ma'qil ibn Yasar, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) say: ""Act according to the Quran, make lawful its lawful things, make unlawful its unlawful things, follow it, and do not disbelieve in anything of it. And whatever is ambiguous to you, refer it to Allah and to those in authority after me, so that they may inform you. And believe in the Torah, the Gospel, the Psalms, and what the prophets were given from their Lord, so that the Quran may heal you and what is in it of clarification; for it is an intercessor whose intercession is accepted, and a credible witness. And for every verse of it there is a light until the Day of Resurrection. Know that I have been given Surah al-Baqarah from the Remembrance, and I have been given Ta Ha and at-Tur from the tablets of Moses, and I have been given the Opening of the Book and the concluding verses of Surah al-Baqarah from a treasure beneath the Throne, and I have been given the Mufassal as a supererogatory gift.""Al-Tabarani narrated it in al-Kabir, and in another narration of his: ""So whatever is ambiguous to you from it, ask the people of knowledge about it, and they will inform you.""It has two chains of narration: in one of them is Abdullah ibn Abi Humayd, and they have consensus on his weakness; and in the other is Imran al-Qattan, whom Ibn Hibban mentioned in al-Thiqat, but the rest weakened him."
"783",""
"784",""
"785","On the authority of Ibn Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Whoever walks to the Sultan of Allah on earth to humiliate him, Allah will humiliate his neck, along with what is stored up for him in the Hereafter."" Musaddad added: ""And the Sultan of Allah on earth is: the Book of Allah, the Exalted, and the Sunnah of His Prophet (peace be upon him).""Al-Tabarani narrated it in Al-Kabir, and in its chain is Hussein ibn Qays Abu Ali al-Rahabi. Al-Bukhari, Ahmad, and a group of scholars weakened him. A man called Abu Mihsan claimed that he was a truthful man. I said: And who is Abu Mihsan alongside these [scholars]?!"
"786",""
"787","On the authority of Abdullah ibn Umar, from the Prophet — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him — he said: ""The Jews were asked about Moses, so they exaggerated about him, added and subtracted until they disbelieved. The Christians were asked about Jesus, so they exaggerated about him, added and subtracted until they disbelieved in him. And indeed, hadiths will spread about me. So whatever comes to you from my hadith, then read the Book of Allah and compare it. Whatever agrees with the Book of Allah, then I said it. And whatever does not agree with the Book of Allah, then I did not say it."" Al-Tabarani narrated it in Al-Kabir, and in its chain is Abu Hazir Abd al-Malik ibn Abd Rabbihi, and he is munkar al-hadith (rejected in hadith)."
"788",""
"789",""
"790",""
"791",""
"792",""
"793",""
"794",""
"795",""
"796",""
"797",""
"798","On the authority of Ibn Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) delivered a sermon and said: ""Verily, Allah has given every rightful person their right. Behold, Allah has prescribed obligations, established Sunnahs, set limits, permitted the lawful, prohibited the unlawful, and legislated the religion, making it easy, tolerant, and vast, and He did not make it constricted. Behold, there is no faith for one who has no trustworthiness, and no religion for one who has no covenant. Whoever breaks Allah's covenant, He will seek him out. Whoever breaks my covenant, I will dispute with him. Whoever I dispute with, I will prevail over him. Whoever breaks my covenant will not attain my intercession, and will not arrive at the Pool. Behold, Allah has not permitted killing except in three cases: an apostate after faith, an adulterer after being married, or a killer of a soul who is killed for his killing. Have I conveyed (the message)?""Narrated by Al-Tabarani in Al-Kabir, and in its chain is Hussein ibn Qays, surnamed Hanash, and he is abandoned in hadith."
"799",""
"800",""
"801",""
"802",""
"803",""
"804",""
"805","On the authority of Umar ibn al-Khattab, who mentioned a story saying: ""I set out and copied a scripture from the People of the Book, then I came with it in a leather vessel. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said to me: 'What is this in your hand, O Umar?' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, a scripture I copied to increase our knowledge with it.' The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) became angry until his cheeks reddened. Then the call to congregational prayer was made. The Ansar said: 'Has your Prophet (peace be upon him) become angry? To arms, to arms!' They came until they surrounded the pulpit of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him). He said: 'O people, I have been given the comprehensive words and their conclusions, and they have been summarized for me concisely. I have brought them to you pure and clear, so do not deviate, and let not the deviators deceive you.' Umar said: So I stood up and said: 'I am pleased with Allah as Lord, with Islam as religion, and with you as Messenger.' Then the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) descended.""Abu Ya'la narrated it, and in its chain is Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq; Ahmad and a group of scholars weakened him. The hadith comes with its full story in the chapter on following the predecessors (salaf)."
"806","On the authority of Abdullah ibn Thabit, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab came to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I passed by a brother of mine from Banu Qurayzah, and he wrote for me comprehensive passages from the Torah. Shall I not present them to you?"" The face of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) changed. Abdullah — meaning ibn Thabit — said: I said: ""Do you not see what is on the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)?"" Umar said: ""We are pleased with Allah as Lord, with Islam as religion, and with Muhammad (peace be upon him) as Messenger."" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was relieved. He said: ""By the One in Whose hand is the soul of Muhammad, if Moses were to appear among you and you followed him and left me, you would go astray. You are my portion among the nations, and I am your portion among the Prophets."" Narrated by Ahmad and al-Tabarani, and its narrators are the narrators of the Sahih, except that it contains Jabir al-Ju'fi, and he is weak."
"807",""
"808","On the authority of Jabir ibn Abdullah: Umar ibn al-Khattab came to the Prophet (peace be upon him) with a book he had obtained from some of the People of the Book. He read it to the Prophet (peace be upon him), who became angry and said: ""Are you delving into it, O son of al-Khattab?! By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, I have brought it to you white and pure. Do not ask them about anything, for they may inform you of truth and you deny it, or falsehood and you believe it. And by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, if Moses were alive among you, he would have no choice but to follow me.""Narrated by Ahmad, Abu Ya'la, and al-Bazzar. In its chain is Mujalid ibn Sa'id, whom Ahmad, Yahya ibn Sa'id, and others have weakened."
"809","Also on the authority of Jabir, he said: Umar copied a book from the Torah in Arabic and brought it to the Prophet. He began to read it, and the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) changed. A man from the Ansar said: ""Woe to you, O son of al-Khattab! Do you not see the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Do not ask the People of the Book about anything; for they will not guide you and they have gone astray. And you will either belie the truth or affirm falsehood. By Allah, if Moses were among you, it would not be lawful for him except to follow me."" Narrated by al-Bazzar, and with Ahmad is part of it. In it is Jabir al-Ja'fi, and he is weak, accused of lying."
"810","On the authority of Abu al-Darda', he said: ""Umar came with sheets from the Torah to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, these are sheets from the Torah which I took from a brother of mine from Banu Zuraq.' The face of the Messenger of Allah changed. Then Abdullah ibn Zayd, the one who was shown the Adhan, said: 'May Allah distort your mind! Do you not see the expression on the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)?' Umar said: 'We are pleased with Allah as Lord, with Islam as religion, with Muhammad as Prophet, and with the Quran as Imam.' The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was relieved. Then he said: 'By Him in Whose hand is the soul of Muhammad, if Moses were among you and you followed him and left me, you would have gone far astray. You are my portion among the nations, and I am your portion among the Prophets.'""Narrated by al-Tabarani in al-Mu'jam al-Kabir. Its chain includes Abu 'Amir al-Qasim ibn Muhammad al-Asadi, and I have not seen anyone who has authenticated him, and the rest of its narrators are trustworthy."
"811",""
"812","Anas ibn Sirin said: ""I was with Ibn Umar - may Allah have mercy on him - at Arafat. When it was time to depart, I departed with him until he reached the Imam, and he prayed with him the first (Dhuhr) and Asr prayers. Then he stood, and I and some companions of mine, until the Imam departed (from Arafat), so we departed with him until he reached the narrow pass before the two mountain passes (Mazimayn), where he made his camel kneel. We made our camels kneel, thinking that he wanted to pray. His servant who held his mount said: 'He does not intend to pray, but he mentioned that the Prophet - peace be upon him - when he reached this place, he relieved himself, so he wishes to relieve himself.'"" Narrated by Ahmad, and its narrators are the narrators of the Sahih."
"813",""
"814",""
"815","On the authority of Wābiṣah ibn Maʿbad, the Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), who said: ""I came to the Prophet (peace be upon him) to ask him about righteousness (al-birr) and sin (al-ithm). He said: 'Have you come to ask about righteousness and sin?' I said: 'By the One Who sent you with the truth, I have not come to ask you about anything else.' He said: 'Righteousness is that which the soul feels at ease with and the heart finds tranquility in, even if people give you a verdict (fatwa) about it.'""Narrated by Aḥmad and al-Bazzār. In its chain is Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Sulamī. Al-Bazzār said: al-Asadī from Wābiṣah, and from him Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ, and I did not find anyone who provided his biography."
"816","On the authority of Ayyub ibn Abdullah ibn Makraz, who did not hear it from him, he said: His companions narrated to me, and I saw him, meaning Wabisah ibn Ma'bad al-Asadi. 'Affan said: He narrated it to us more than once, and he did not say ""my companions narrated to me.""He said: ""I came to the Prophet - peace be upon him - while I wanted to not leave anything of righteousness and sin except that I would ask him about it. Around him was a group of Muslims asking him for religious verdicts. So I began stepping over them, and they said: 'Step back, O Wabisah, from the Messenger of Allah.' I said: 'Let me approach him, for he is the most beloved of people to me that I should approach him.' He said: 'Let Wabisah be, come near, O Wabisah,' two or three times.""He said: ""So I drew near to him until I sat before him. He said: 'Shall I inform you or will you ask me?' I said: 'No, rather inform me.' He said: 'You came to ask me about righteousness and sin?' I said: 'Yes.' So he placed his three fingertips, knocking them against my chest, and said: 'O Wabisah, consult your soul, consult your soul' - three times - 'Righteousness is that which the soul finds tranquility in, and sin is that which wavers in your soul and hesitates in your chest, even if people give you verdicts and keep giving you verdicts.'""Narrated by Ahmad and Abu Ya'la, and in it is Ayyub ibn Abdullah ibn Makraz. Ibn 'Adi said: He is not followed up on his hadith, and Ibn Hibban authenticated him."
"817",""
"818",""
"819",""
"820","On the authority of Aisha, that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Six [types of people] I have cursed, and Allah has cursed them—and every Prophet's supplication is answered: the one who adds to the Book of Allah (Mighty and Majestic), the one who denies the Divine Decree of Allah (Mighty and Majestic), the one who violates the sanctity of Allah, the one who makes lawful from my family (Ahl al-Bayt) what Allah has forbidden, and the one who abandons the Sunnah.""Al-Tabarani narrated it in *Al-Mu'jam Al-Kabir*. In its chain is Ubaidullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Mawheb. Yaqub ibn Shaybah said: ""There is weakness in him."" Yahya ibn Ma'in weakened him in one narration, and authenticated him in another. Abu Hatim said: ""His hadith is acceptable (Salih)."" Ibn Hibban authenticated him. And its narrators are the narrators of the *Sahih* (i.e., meeting the criteria of Bukhari and Muslim)."
"821","On the authority of Amr ibn Shaghwa al-Yafi'i, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Seven [types of people] I have cursed — and every prophet's supplication is answered: the one who adds to the Book of Allah, the one who denies the decree of Allah, the one who violates the sanctity of Allah, the one who makes lawful from my family (Ahl al-Bayt) what Allah has forbidden, the one who abandons my Sunnah, the one who monopolizes the spoils of war (fay'), and the tyrant who uses his authority to honor whom Allah has humiliated and humiliate whom Allah has honored — Mighty and Majestic is He."" Narrated by al-Tabarani in al-Kabir, and in its chain is Ibn Lahi'ah, who is weak, and Abu Ma'shar al-Himyari, whom I have not seen anyone mention."
"822",""
"823",""
"824",""
"825",""
"826",""
"827",""
"828",""
"829",""
"830",""
"831",""
"832","On the authority of Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, he said: ""Verily Allah - Mighty and Majestic - looked into the hearts of the servants, and found the heart of Muhammad - peace be upon him - to be the best of the hearts of the servants, so He chose him for Himself and sent him with His messages. Then He looked into the hearts of the servants [after the heart of Muhammad], and found the hearts of his companions to be the best of the hearts of the servants, so He made them ministers of His Prophet - peace be upon him - fighting for his religion. Therefore, whatever the Muslims deem good is good with Allah, and whatever the Muslims deem evil is evil with Allah."" Narrated by Ahmad, al-Bazzar, and al-Tabarani in al-Kabir, and its narrators are trustworthy."
"833",""
"834",""
"835",""
"836","And on the authority of Mu'adh ibn Jabal, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he intended to send Mu'adh to Yemen, consulted a group of his Companions, among them Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman, Ali, Talhah, al-Zubayr, and Usayd ibn Hudayr. So he consulted them. Abu Bakr said: ""Had you not consulted us, we would not have spoken."" He said: ""Indeed, in matters not revealed to me, I am but one of you."" So the people spoke, each man giving his opinion. He said: ""What do you think, O Mu'adh?"" I said: ""I see what Abu Bakr said."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Indeed, Allah hates above His heavens that Abu Bakr should err.""Narrated by al-Tabarani in *al-Mu'jam al-Kabir*. In its chain is Abu al-Atuf; I have not seen anyone who has documented him narrating from al-Wadin ibn Ata, and the rest of its narrators are trustworthy."
"837","It was narrated from Ibn Abbas that he said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was walking among the palm trees in Medina, and the people began to say: 'It yields such-and-such a wasq (a measure of volume).' The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'It yields such-and-such.' They said: 'Allah and His Messenger have spoken the truth.' The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'I am only a human being like you. Whatever I tell you about Allah is the truth, but whatever I say from my own opinion, then I am only a human being; I may be right and I may be wrong.'""Al-Bazzar narrated it, and its chain is good (hasan), except that Ismail ibn Abdullah al-Asbahani, the shaykh of Al-Bazzar, I have not seen anyone who has written a biography for him."
"838",""
"839",""
"840",""
"841",""
"842",""
"843","And on the authority of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, he said: ""Suspect personal opinion in matters of religion; for I found myself opposing the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) while not falling short of the truth, and that was on the Day of Abū Jandal. The document was before the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and the people of Mecca, so he said: 'Write: In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful.' They said: 'Do you think that if we did that we would have believed you in what you say? But write: In Your name, O Allah.' So the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) agreed, but I refused them until he said to me: 'O ʿUmar, you see that I have agreed and you refuse?' He said: So I agreed.""Narrated by Abū Yaʿlā, and its narrators are trustworthy, even though among them is Mubārak ibn Faḍālah."
"844","On the authority of Ibn Abbas, he said: ""When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) returned from the Battle of Hunayn, there was revealed to him {When comes the Help of Allah and the Victory} [Surah An-Nasr: 1] to the end of the Surah. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'O Ali ibn Abi Talib, O Fatimah daughter of Muhammad, the Help of Allah and the Victory has come, and I have seen people entering the religion of Allah in crowds, so glory be to my Lord and praise be to Him, and I seek His forgiveness; indeed He is Ever-Accepting of Repentance. And O Ali, there will be after me among the believers Jihad (striving).' He (Ali) said: 'On what basis do we fight the believers who say ""We believe in Allah""?' He said: 'On the innovations in the religion, when they act based on opinion, and there is no opinion in the religion; the religion is only from the Lord: His command and His prohibition.' Ali said: 'O Messenger of Allah, what do you think if a matter arises for us in which no Quran was revealed, and no Sunnah from you has passed?' He said: 'You make it a consultation among the worshippers from the believers, and you do not judge it by a single opinion; for if I were to appoint a successor, none would be more deserving than you, due to your precedence in Islam, your closeness to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) [your being his son-in-law], and you have the Mistress of the Women of the Believers, and before that was the trial of Abu Talib towards me, and the Quran was revealed while I was keen to maintain his rights in his offspring.'""Narrated by Al-Tabarani in Al-Mu'jam Al-Kabir, and in it is Abdullah ibn Kaysan; Al-Bukhari said: ""Munkar al-Hadith"" (rejected/denounced hadith)."
"845",""
"846",""
"847",""
"848",""
"849",""
"850",""
"851",""
"852",""
"853",""
"854","On the authority of 'Amr ibn Salamah, who said: We were sitting at the door of Ibn Mas'ud between Maghrib and 'Isha', when Abu Musa came and said: ""Come out to us, O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman."" So Ibn Mas'ud came out, and Abu Musa said: ""What brought you at this hour?"" He said: ""By Allah, nothing except that I saw something that frightened me, and indeed it is good; and by Allah it frightened me, and indeed it is good: a group of people sitting in the mosque, and a man saying: 'Glorify Allah such-and-such times, praise Allah such-and-such times.'"" He said: So 'Abdullah (Ibn Mas'ud) went forth, and we went with him until he reached them. He said: ""How quickly you have gone astray, while the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) are still alive, his wives are still present, his garments and vessels have not yet decayed. Count your evil deeds, for I guarantee by Allah that He will count your good deeds.""Al-Tabarani narrated it in Al-Mu'jam Al-Kabir, and in its chain is Mujalid ibn Sa'id; Al-Nasa'i deemed him trustworthy, while Al-Bukhari, Ahmad ibn Hanbal, and Yahya weakened him."
"855","On the authority of Abu al-Bakhtari, he said: It reached Abdullah ibn Mas'ud that some people were sitting between Maghrib and Isha prayers saying: ""Say such-and-such, say such-and-such."" Abdullah said: ""If they do that, inform me."" So when they sat, they came to him, and he went with them. He sat down wearing a burnus (a hooded cloak). They began their tasbih (glorification of Allah). Abdullah removed the burnus from his head and said: ""I am Abdullah ibn Mas'ud."" The people fell silent. He said: ""You have indeed introduced an innovation unjustly, or else the Companions of Muhammad (peace be upon him) have gone astray."" Amr ibn Utbah ibn Farqad said: ""I seek forgiveness from Allah, O Ibn Mas'ud, and I repent to Him."" So he ordered them to disperse.He said: And Ibn Mas'ud saw two circles in the mosque of Kufa, so he stood between them and said: ""Which of you two existed before the other?"" One of them said: ""We did."" He said to the other: ""Stand up and join them."" So he made them one.Al-Tabarani narrated it in Al-Kabir, and in its chain is Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, who is trustworthy but his memory became confused.And in some of the authentic abbreviated chains of Al-Tabarani: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud came wearing a hood and said: ""Whoever knows me knows me, and whoever does not know me, I am Abdullah ibn Mas'ud. You are indeed more guided than Muhammad (peace be upon him) and his Companions, or you are clinging to the tail of misguidance.""And in a narration of Ata ibn al-Sa'ib: Ibn Mas'ud said: ""If you follow these people, they have indeed preceded you by a far, clear distance. And if you take right and left, you have indeed gone far astray."""
"856",""
"857","And on the authority of Khālid ibn ʿUrfuṭah, he said: ""I was sitting with ʿUmar when a man from ʿAbd al-Qays, whose dwelling was in al-Sūs, was brought to him. ʿUmar said to him: 'Are you so-and-so, son of so-and-so, the ʿAbdī?' He said: 'Yes.' So he struck him with a staff he had with him. The man said: 'What is my crime, O Commander of the Faithful?' ʿUmar said to him: 'Sit down.' So he sat down. Then he recited to him: 'In the Name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. {Alif-Lām-Rā. These are the Verses of the Clear Book. Indeed, We have sent it down as an Arabic Qurʾān that you may understand. We relate to you the best of stories through Our revelation of this Qurʾān to you, though you were, before it, among the heedless}' [Yūsuf: 1–3]. He recited them to him three times and struck him three times. The man said: 'What is my crime, O Commander of the Faithful?' He said: 'You are the one who copied the Books of Dāniyāl?' He said: 'Command me with your command, I will follow it.' He said: 'Go and erase it with hot water and white wool, then do not read it yourself nor teach it to anyone among the people. For by Allah, if it reaches me that you have read it or taught it to anyone among the people, I will surely punish you severely.' Then he said to him: 'Sit down.' So he sat before him.He said: 'I once went and copied a book from the People of the Book, then brought it in a leather vessel. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""What is this in your hand, O ʿUmar?"" I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, a book I copied to increase our knowledge with knowledge."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ became angry until his cheeks reddened. Then the call for congregational prayer was made. The Anṣār said: ""Has your Prophet ﷺ become angry? To arms, to arms!"" They came until they surrounded the pulpit of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: ""O people, I have been given the comprehensive words (Jawāmiʿ al-Kalim) and their conclusions, and they have been summarized for me concisely. I have brought them to you white and pure, so do not deviate (lā tatahawwakū), and let not those who deviate (al-mutahawwikūna) deceive you."" ʿUmar said: So I stood up and said: ""I am pleased with Allah as Lord, with Islam as religion, and with you as Messenger."" Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ descended.""Narrated by Abū Yaʿlā, and in its chain is ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Isḥāq al-Wāsiṭī; Aḥmad and a group of scholars weakened him."
"858",""
"859",""
"860",""
"861",""
"862","On the authority of Abdullah ibn Amr, from the Prophet - may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him -: ""There was a female dog among the Children of Israel that was pregnant. Her owners hosted a guest, so she said: 'I will not bark at tonight's guest.' Then her puppy howled in her womb. So it was revealed to a man among them that the likeness of this female dog is like a nation that will come after you, whose foolish ones will prevail over their scholars."" Narrated by al-Tabarani in al-Awsat, and Ahmad narrated similarly except that in Ahmad's hadith: ""Their foolish ones will overcome their forbearing ones."" - And it appears in [the chapter on] Tribulations, and its chain includes Shu'ayb ibn Safwan; Ibn Hibban authenticated him, and Yahya weakened him. And Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, who had become confused [in his later years]."
"863",""
"864",""
"865",""
"866",""
"867",""
"868",""
"869",""
"870",""
"871","On the authority of Abu Tamima, from Jundub ibn Abdullah al-Azdi, a Companion of the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him), who said: ""I set out with him to Basra until we came to a place called the House of the Poor, which is from Basra at a distance like a stage. He said: 'Did you used to study the Quran with anyone?' I said: 'Yes.' He said: 'Then when we reach Basra, bring them to me.' So I brought him Salih ibn Misrah, Abu Bilal and his grandfather, and Nafi ibn al-Azraq, who were in my opinion at that time among the most virtuous people of Basra. He began to narrate to me from the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him). Jundub said: The Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'The likeness of the scholar who teaches people good but forgets himself is like that of a lamp: it gives light to people but burns itself.' And the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'Let not a handful of blood shed unjustly come between any of you and Paradise while he looks at its gates.' The people spoke, mentioning enjoining good and forbidding evil, while he remained silent listening to them. Then he said: 'I have never seen a day like this; a people more deserving of salvation if they are truthful.' Al-Tabarani narrated it in Al-Kabir, and it has a chain that will come in the chapter on fighting the people of rebellion, and its narrators are trustworthy."""
"872",""
"873","On the authority of ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, he said: “The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) sent me to a tribe of Qays to teach them the laws of Islam. I found a people like wild camels, their gazes fixed upward, having no concern except for a sheep or a camel. So I returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and he said: ‘O ʿAmmār, what did you do?’ I narrated to him the story of the people and informed him of the heedlessness in them. He said: ‘O ʿAmmār, shall I not inform you of something more astonishing than them? A people who knew what those people were ignorant of, then they became heedless just like their heedlessness.’”Narrated by al‑Bazzār and al‑Ṭabarānī in al‑Kabīr, and in its chain is ʿAbbād ibn Aḥmad al‑ʿAzramī. Al‑Dāraquṭnī said: “Matrūk (abandoned/rejected narrator).”"
"874",""
"875",""
"876","On the authority of Al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""The religion will prevail until the merchants surpass it, and the seas will be traversed in the cause of Allah. Then after you will come a people who recite the Quran, saying: 'We have recited the Quran. Who is more proficient in recitation than us? Who has more understanding than us? Who is more knowledgeable than us?' Then he turned to his companions and said: 'Is there any good in those people?' They said: 'No.' He said: 'Those are from you, from this nation, and they are the fuel of the Fire.'""Narrated by Abu Ya'la, Al-Bazzar, and Al-Tabarani in Al-Kabir. In its chain is Musa ibn Ubaydah Al-Rabadhi, and he is weak."
"877","On the authority of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Islam will prevail until merchants travel across the sea, and until horses wade in the cause of Allah. Then a people will appear who recite the Qur'an, saying: 'Who among us is the best reciter? Who among us is the most knowledgeable? Who among us has the deepest understanding?' Then he said to his companions: 'Is there any good in those people?' They said: 'Allah and His Messenger know best.' He said: 'Those are from you, from this nation, and those are the fuel of the Fire.'""Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī in al-Awsaṭ and al-Bazzār, and the narrators of al-Bazzār are trustworthy."
"878","On the authority of Umm al-Fadl and Abdullah ibn Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him): ""He stood up one night in Mecca during the night and said: 'O Allah, have I conveyed (the message)?' three times. Umar ibn al-Khattab stood up, and he was one who frequently supplicated (awwah), and said: 'O Allah, yes, you have exhorted, striven, and advised sincerely.' [So he (the Prophet) said:] 'Faith will surely prevail until disbelief is returned to its places, and the seas will surely be traversed for the sake of Islam, and there will surely come upon people a time when they will learn the Quran, they will learn it and recite it, and they will say: ""We have recited and learned, so who is better than us?"" Is there any harm in those people?' They said: 'O Messenger of Allah, who are those?' He said: 'They are from you, and they are the fuel of the Fire.'""Narrated by al-Tabarani in al-Kabir, and its narrators are trustworthy, except that Hind bint al-Harith al-Khath'amiyyah al-Tabi'iyyah, I have not seen anyone who authenticated her nor criticized her."
"879",""
"880",""
"881",""
"882","On the authority of Mu'adh ibn Jabal, from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him): ""Beware of three things: the slip of a scholar, the argumentation of a hypocrite with the Quran, and a worldly life that severs your necks. As for the slip of a scholar: if he is guided, do not follow him blindly in your religion; and if he slips, do not cut off your hopes from him. As for the argumentation of a hypocrite with the Quran: indeed the Quran has signposts like the signposts of the road; so what you recognize, take it, and what you deny, refer it to its Knower. As for the worldly life that severs your necks: whoever Allah places richness in his heart, then he is rich.""Al-Tabarani narrated it in Al-Awsat. And 'Amr ibn Murrah did not hear from Mu'adh. And Abdullah ibn Salih, the scribe of Al-Layth, was authenticated by 'Abd al-Malik ibn Shu'ayb ibn Al-Layth and Yahya in a narration from him, and Ahmad and a group weakened him."
"883",""
"884",""
"885",""
"886",""
"887",""
"888",""
"889",""
"890",""
"891",""
"892","On the authority of Ghudayf ibn al-Ḥārith al-Thumālī, he said: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Marwān sent for me and said: ""O Abū Sulaymān, we have united the people upon two matters."" He said: ""And what are they?"" He said: ""Raising hands on the pulpits on Friday, and storytelling after Fajr and ʿAṣr."" He said: ""[Verily] they are like your innovations in my view, and I will not comply with you in anything of them."" He said: ""Why?"" He said: ""Because the Prophet ﷺ said: 'No people introduce an innovation except that its like is lifted from the Sunnah, so holding fast to a Sunnah is better than introducing an innovation.'"" Narrated by Aḥmad and al-Bazzār, and in its chain is Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Maryam, and he is a rejected narrator."
"893",""
"894",""
"895",""
"896",""
"897",""
"898",""
"899",""
"900",""
"901","On the authority of Al-Harith ibn Mu'awiyah that he rode to Umar ibn al-Khattab to ask him about three matters. He said: He arrived in Medina and Umar asked him: ""What brought you?"" He said: ""To ask you about three matters."" He said: ""And what are they?"" He said: ""Perhaps I and a woman are in a narrow building, then the prayer time arrives. If I pray and she is beside me, and if she prays behind me, she would have to leave the building."" He said: ""Place a garment as a screen between you and her, then pray beside her if you wish."" And about the two rak'ahs after Asr. He said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) forbade me from them."" He said: ""And about storytelling."" He said: ""Whatever you wish"" — as if he disliked preventing him. He said: ""I only wanted to conform to your opinion."" He said: ""I fear for you that you will tell stories and become arrogant in your soul, then tell stories and become arrogant in your soul, until it appears to you that you are above them in status like the Pleiades, then Allah will place you beneath their feet on the Day of Resurrection to the extent of that."" Narrated by Ahmad. Al-Harith ibn Mu'awiyah al-Kindi; Ibn Hibban authenticated him and others narrated from him, and the rest of its narrators are from the narrators of the Sahih."
"902",""
"903",""
"904",""
"905",""
"906",""
"907",""
"908",""
"909",""
"910",""
"911","On the authority of Abu Umamah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) went out to a storyteller who was narrating, so he fell silent. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Continue narrating, for my sitting from early morning until the sun rises is more beloved to me than freeing four slaves, and my sitting after Asr until the sun sets is more beloved to me than freeing four slaves."" Narrated by Ahmad and Al-Tabarani in Al-Kabir, except that Al-Tabarani's wording is: ""I narrate, for my sitting in this place from the time the Fajr prayer is performed until the sun rises"" — then he mentioned the hadith. Its narrators are trustworthy, except that it contains Abu Al-Ja'd from Abu Umamah; if he is Al-Ghutafani, then he is among the narrators of Sahih, and if he is someone else, then I do not know him."
"912",""
"913",""
"914","On the authority of the four Abdullahs: Abdullah ibn Umar, Abdullah ibn Abbas, Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, and Abdullah ibn Amr, they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""The storyteller awaits wrath, the listener awaits mercy, the merchant awaits provision, the hoarder awaits curse, the wailing woman and the women around her — upon them is the curse of Allah, the angels, and all mankind."" Narrated by al-Tabarani in al-Mu'jam al-Kabir, and in its chain is Bishr ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari from Abdullah ibn Mujahid ibn Jabr, and I have not seen anyone who mentioned them."
"915","On the authority of Al-Sha'bi, he said: Aisha said to Ibn Abi Al-Sa'ib, the storyteller of the people of Medina, three times: ""You will comply with me in these matters, or I will dispute with you."" He said: ""What are they? Rather, I will comply with you, O Mother of the Believers."" She said: ""Avoid rhymed prose (saj') in supplication; for the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and his companions did not do that. And preach to the people every Friday once; if you refuse, then twice; if you refuse, then three times. And do not cause the people to err in [recitation of] this Book. And do not let me find you coming to a people while they are in their conversation, so you cut off their conversation. But leave them; then when they urge you to it and command you to do it, then narrate to them.""Narrated by Ahmad, and its narrators are the narrators of the Sahih, and narrated by Abu Ya'la similarly."
"916",""
"917",""
"918",""
"919",""
"920",""
"921",""
"922","On the authority of Samurah ibn Jundub, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: Beware of extremism and arrogance, for many of the Children of Israel went to extremes, until a short woman would make two wooden clogs, stuff them, then insert her feet into them, then she would approach a tall woman and walk with her, and then she would have become equal to her or even taller than her. Al-Tabarani narrated it in Al-Kabir, and its chain includes Marwan ibn Ja'far; Ibn Abi Hatim deemed him trustworthy, Al-Azdi said they criticize him, and Al-Dhahabi said he has a version containing munkar (rejected) narrations."
"923",""
"924",""
"925",""
"926",""
"927",""
"928",""
"929",""
"930",""
"931",""
"932","On the authority of Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""The children of Noah are: Shem, Ham, and Japheth. Shem begat the Arabs, the Persians, and the Romans, and goodness is among them. Japheth begat Gog and Magog, the Turks, and the Slavs, and there is no goodness among them. Ham begat the Copts, the Berbers, and the Sudanese.""Al-Bazzar narrated it, and in its chain is Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn Sinan al-Ruhawi from his father. As for Muhammad: Ibn Hibban deemed him trustworthy; Abu Hatim said: ""He is truthful""; Yahya ibn Ma'in and Al-Bukhari weakened him. As for Yazid ibn Sinan: Abu Hatim deemed him trustworthy, saying: ""His status is that of truthfulness."" Al-Bukhari said: ""He is close in hadith (i.e., his hadith are near to being acceptable)."" Yahya and a group weakened him."
"933",""
"934","On the authority of Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him — she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him — saying: ""Ma'ad son of Adnan son of [Udd son of] Udad son of Zayd son of Bara' son of A'raq al-Thara."" She said: Then the Messenger of Allah — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him — would say: ""He destroyed 'Ad and Thamud and the Companions of the Rass, and many generations between them that none knows except Allah."" So Umm Salamah would say: ""Ma'ad is Ma'ad, and Adnan is Adnan, and Udad is Udad, and Zayd son of Humaysa', and Bara': Tibt, and A'raq al-Thara is Isma'il son of Ibrahim."" Narrated by al-Tabarani in al-Saghir, and in its chain is: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Imran from the descendants of 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf, and al-Bukhari and a group have weakened him, and Ibn Hibban mentioned him in al-Thiqat."
"935",""
"936","And on the authority of Ibn Abbas: ""A man asked the Prophet - may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him - about Saba': What is it? A man, a woman, or a land? He said: 'Rather, he is a man. Ten were born to him. Six of them settled in Yemen, and four settled in Sham. As for the Yemenis: Madhhij, Kinda, Al-Azd, Al-Ash'ariyyun, Anmar, and Himyar - all of them Arabs. And as for the Shamis: Lakhm, Judham, Amilah, and Ghassan.'""

Ahmad and Al-Tabarani in Al-Kabir narrated it, and in its chain is Ibn Lahi'ah, and he is weak.

I say: And the hadith of Yazid ibn Husayn will come in Surah Saba', and it is more authentic than this one."
"937","On the authority of Amr ibn Murrah al-Juhani, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) say: ""Whoever is here from Ma'add, let him stand up."" So I stood up, and he said: ""Sit down."" He did this three times; each time I stood up, he said: ""Sit down."" When it was the third time, I said: ""From whom are we, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""You — O people of Quda'ah — are from Himyar."" Amr said: So I concealed this hadith for twenty years.It was narrated by Ahmad, Abu Ya'la, al-Bazzar, and al-Tabarani in al-Kabir, and he has other chains for it. In some of them: ""I said: O Messenger of Allah, from whom are we? He said: 'You are from the freed hand and the wholesome morsel; from Himyar.'"" And in its chain is Ibn Lahi'ah."
"938","Also from ʿAmr ibn Murra al-Juhanī, who said: ""While we were with the Prophet ﷺ, he said: 'Whoever is here from Maʿadd, let him stand up.' ʿAmr ibn Murra stood up, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'Sit down.' So he sat down. Then he said: 'Whoever is here from Maʿadd, let him stand up.' ʿAmr ibn Murra stood up, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: 'Sit down.' Then he said: 'Whoever is here from Maʿadd, let him stand up.' ʿAmr ibn Murra stood up, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'Sit down.' He said: 'O Messenger of Allah, from whom are we?' He said: 'You are from Quḍāʿa ibn Mālik ibn Ḥimyar, the known lineage, not the rejected one.'""ʿAmr said: ""I kept this hadith secret until the days of Muʿāwiya ibn Abī Sufyān. He sent for me and said: 'Would you like to ascend the pulpit and mention that Quḍāʿa is the son of Maʿadd ibn ʿAdnān, on condition that I give you the tax revenue of the two Iraqs and Egypt for my lifetime?' ʿAmr ibn Murra said: 'Yes.' He called for the congregational prayer, so the people gathered. ʿAmr came, stepping over the necks of the people, until he ascended the pulpit. He praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: 'O people, whoever knows me knows me, and whoever does not know me, I am ʿAmr ibn Murra al-Juhanī. Verily, Muʿāwiya ibn Abī Sufyān invited me to ascend the pulpit and mention that Quḍāʿa is the son of Maʿadd ibn ʿAdnān. Nay:We are the sons of the gray-haired, white-haired sheikh... Quḍāʿa ibn Mālik ibn ḤimyarThe known lineage, not the rejected one.'Then he descended. Muʿāwiya said to him: 'Away with you, O traitor!' three times. He said: 'It is as you saw, O Commander of the Faithful.' His son Zuhayr followed him and said: 'O father, what harm would there have been if you had obeyed the Commander of the Faithful and he gave you the tax revenue of the two Iraqs and Egypt for his lifetime?' He began to say:'Had I obeyed you, O Zuhayr, you would have clothed me... among the people with a conspicuous mantle of shame.Qaḥṭān is our father to whom we are attributed... and Abū Khuzayma Khindif ibn Nizār.Is the error of a night whose leaves have fallen... among the people more excusable, or the error of daytime?Shall we sell our father to whom we are attributed... for a father of livelihood, absent and hidden?That is a trade we do not accept the like of... gold sold for dross and dust.'Al-Ṭabarānī narrated it in al-Kabīr, and in it is Dalhāth ibn Dāwūd. Al-Azdī said: 'His hadith from his forefathers is not authentic.' And this is from his hadith from his forefathers."""
"939","Al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah narrated from his father who said: ""I attended the Prophet — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him — while he was saying: 'Whoever is here from Ma'add, let him stand up.' So 'Amr ibn Murrah al-Juhani stood up. The Prophet — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him — said to him: 'Sit down.' He did that three times. Then the Prophet — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him — said: 'Quda'ah is from Himyar.'"" Narrated by al-Tabarani in al-Kabir, and its narrators are the narrators of al-Sahih, except for Muhammad ibn Abi 'Ubayd al-Darawardi, the father of 'Abd al-'Aziz, for I have not seen anyone who has written his biography."
"940","On the authority of Abu Ushanah, who said: I heard Uqbah ibn Amir saying while he was on the pulpit: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) arrived in Medina while I was tending some sheep of mine. I left them and went to him. I said: 'Will you pledge allegiance to me, O Messenger of Allah?' He said: 'Who are you from?' I informed him, so the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'Which is more beloved to you: the pledge of migration (hijrah) or the Bedouin pledge?' I said: 'The pledge of migration,' so he pledged allegiance to me. Then one day the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'Whoever is here from Ma'add, let him stand up.' I stood up, and he said: 'Sit down.' Then he said: 'Whoever is here from Ma'add, let him stand up.' I stood up, and he said: 'Sit down.' Then he said it a third time, so I stood up, and he said: 'Sit down.' I said: 'From whom are we, O Messenger of Allah?' He said: 'You are from Quda'ah ibn Malik ibn Himyar.'""Narrated by al-Tabarani in al-Kabir, and in its chain is Ibn Lahi'ah, who is weak, and his shaykh is Ma'ruf ibn Suwayd, I have not seen anyone who has provided a biography for him."
"941","Al-Jafshish al-Kindi said: ""A group from Kindah came to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and said: 'You are from us,' and they claimed him. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'We do not follow our mother [in lineage] nor do we disown our father; we are from the descendants of al-Nadr ibn Kinanah.'""Al-Tabarani narrated it in al-Kabir and al-Saghir. In its chain is Isma'il ibn Amr al-Bajali. Abu Hatim and al-Daraqutni weakened him, while Ibn Hibban authenticated him. The rest of its narrators are trustworthy.I say: Much of what pertains to lineages, dates of death, names, and kunyahs will come at the end of the virtues of the Companions (may Allah be pleased with them)."
"942",""
