"963","On the authority of Nu'aym ibn Dajajah, who said: I was sitting with 'Ali when Abu Mas'ud arrived. 'Ali said: ""Farrukh has come,"" so he sat down. 'Ali said: ""Do you issue fatwas to people?"" He replied: ""Yes, and I inform them [at this moment] that the latter [times] are worse."" 'Ali said: ""Then tell me, did you hear anything from him [the Prophet]?"" He said: ""Yes, I heard him say: 'A hundred years will not pass over the people while there remains on the earth a blinking eye.'"" 'Ali said: ""Your backside missed the pit, and you erred in your first fatwa. He only said that concerning those who were present at that time. Is ease [or prosperity] only after the hundred [years]?""Narrated by Abu Ya'la, and its narrators are trustworthy as well."
"964","On the authority of Anas ibn Malik, he said: ""The boldest of people in questioning the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) were the Bedouins. A Bedouin came to him and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, when is the Hour?' He did not answer him with anything until he came to the mosque, prayed, and lightened the prayer. Then he turned to the Bedouin and said: 'Where is the questioner about the Hour?' Sa'd passed by him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'Indeed, this one will live long until he consumes his lifespan; not a single eye among you will remain blinking.'""Narrated by Abu Ya'la.I said: Anas has a narration in the Sahih: ""If this one lives until he completes his lifespan, he will not die until the Hour is established."" And this hadith is clearer, even though it contains Sufyan ibn Waki', and he is weak."
