"974","Abd al-Malik ibn Sallam said: I said to Abd al-Khayr, ""How many years have passed over you?"" He said, ""One hundred and twenty years."" I said, ""Do you remember anything from the affairs of the pre-Islamic period?"" He said, ""Yes, we were in the land of Yemen, and a letter from the Messenger of Allah came to us calling people to a vast good. My father was among those who went out, and I was a young boy. When my father returned, he said to my mother, 'Order this cooking pot to be poured out for the dogs; for we have become Muslims.'""Narrated by Abu Ya'la, and its narrators are trustworthy.I say: And much of what relates to history and other matters will come in the latter parts of the virtues of the Companions - may Allah be pleased with them."
"975",""
"976","On the authority of Abu Umamah, who said: ""When it was during the Farewell Pilgrimage, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) stood up, and on that day he was riding behind al-Fadl ibn Abbas on a dark-colored camel. He said: 'O people, acquire knowledge before it is taken away, and before it is raised up.' And Allah the Exalted had already revealed: {O you who have believed, do not ask about things which, if they are shown to you, will distress you. But if you ask about them while the Qur'an is being revealed, they will be shown to you. Allah has pardoned it, and Allah is Forgiving and Forbearing.} [Al-Ma'idah: 101]. He said: And we had disliked much of his questioning, and we avoided that when Allah the Exalted revealed that upon His Prophet (peace be upon him). He said: So we came to a Bedouin, bribed him with a cloak, and he wrapped it around his head. He said: Until I saw its hem protruding over his right eyebrow. He said: Then we said to him: Ask the Prophet (peace be upon him). So he said to him: O Prophet of Allah, how is knowledge taken away from us while the scriptures (mushafs) are among us, and we have learned what is in them, and taught them to our women, children, and servants? He said: The Prophet (peace be upon him) raised his head, and his face reddened with anger. He said: He said: 'Ah, may your mother lose you! And these Jews and Christians have the scriptures among them, yet they did not come to hold onto a single letter of what their prophets brought them. Verily, the departure of knowledge is the departure of its bearers,' three times.""Ahmad and al-Tabarani in al-Kabir narrated it, and Ibn Majah has a portion of it. Al-Tabarani's chain is more authentic; because in Ahmad's chain there is Ali ibn Yazid, who is very weak. And in al-Tabarani's version it comes through routes, in some of which is al-Hajjaj ibn Arta'ah, who is a mudallis (one who practices tadlis) but truthful; his hadith is written, and he is not among those who deliberately lie. And Allah knows best."
"977","On the authority of Ibn 'Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Knowledge is about to be lifted up."" He repeated it three times. Ziyad ibn Labid said: ""O Messenger of Allah, may my father and mother be sacrificed for you, how can knowledge be lifted from us when we have the Book of Allah, we have read it and our children teach it to their children?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) turned to him and said: ""May your mother lose you, O Ziyad ibn Labid! I used to consider you among the jurists of the people of Medina. Are not these Jews in possession of the Torah and the Gospel, yet what did it avail them? Indeed, Allah does not take away knowledge by snatching it away, but He takes it away by taking away its bearers — I think he said — and no scholar from this nation passes away except that it becomes a gap in Islam that will not be filled until the Day of Resurrection.""Al-Bazzar narrated it, and in its chain is Sa'd ibn Sinan. Al-Bukhari, Yahya ibn Ma'in, and a group have weakened him, except that Abu Mus'hir said: ""Sadaqah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Abu Mahdiyy Sa'd ibn Sinan, the muezzin of the people of Hims, narrated to me, and he was trustworthy, reliable."""
"978","On the authority of Awf ibn Malik al-Ashja'i: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) looked at the sky and said: 'This is the time when knowledge will be taken away.' A man from the Ansar, called Ziyad ibn Labid, said to him: 'O Messenger of Allah, how can that be when it has been established and the hearts have memorized it?' The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said to him: 'I used to consider you among the most knowledgeable people of Medina,' then he mentioned the misguidance of the Jews and Christians despite what they have of Allah's Book.""Al-Bazzar narrated it, and in its chain is Abdullah ibn Salih, the scribe of al-Layth. Abd al-Malik ibn Shu'ayb said: ""He was trustworthy, reliable."" And the rest weakened him.Al-Tabarani also narrated it in Al-Mu'jam al-Kabir and added: Jubayr ibn Nufayr said: ""I met Shaddad ibn Aws and narrated to him the hadith of Awf, so he said: 'Awf spoke the truth. Shall I not inform you of the first of that? Humility (khushu') will be taken away, you will not see anyone humble.'"""
